# /etc/models — which models a scoop may be spawned with
#
# `agent --model` and `scoop_scoop` accept a `provider:model` id, so a scoop can
# run on a provider OTHER than the selected one. That moves spend onto another
# account — a work provider rather than a personal one — so it is opt-in here.
#
# Sections are keyed by the SELECTED provider; entries apply while it is selected:
#
#   [<selected-provider>]
#   <provider>:<model>    Allow that model.      <provider>:*   Allow all of them.
#   -<provider>:<model>   Deny it (a deny always beats an allow, in any order).
#
# Defaults, with no file or no section for the selected provider:
#   - The selected provider's OWN models are allowed; never list them. A `-`
#     entry subtracts one.
#   - Every OTHER provider's models are denied. Rejections quote the line to add.
#
# The model picker and `models` hide only what a `-` entry denies — applying the
# allow-list there would hide every other account and make switching providers
# impossible. Switching your account is your call; spawning against another one
# is what is gated.
#
# Edits apply immediately. Writing this file needs a human approval (the
# `Write /etc/models` rule in /etc/sudoers) because an agent that could rewrite
# it could authorize its own spend. Reads are ungated.
#
# Example — while `adobe` is selected, allow OpenRouter except one model, and
# keep Adobe's own Opus 5 off-limits (it leaves the picker too):
#
# [adobe]
# openrouter:*
# -openrouter:openai/o3-pro
# -adobe:claude-opus-5
